body:
[A10] **Lane for #640, stage 1: the Mark II area plan.** Executor [A540], branch `640-mark2-plan` from dev `e6172750`. Reviewers [R492] (internal) and [R493] (external) review the plan document. This stage is measurement and planning only. Milestone 12 is due 2026-12-15, before P3 (owner, 2026-10-05).

**Target.** The routed shipping image uses at most 38,040 LUT (NFR-RES-01, 60 % of `xc7a100t`). Plan for a margin of about 5 %. The image meets the build's timing gate at the shipping clock. Function is unchanged.

**Items:**
1. **Baseline at dev.** Record LUT, FF, LUTRAM, BRAM and DSP per hierarchy, deep enough to name each protocol engine, from the routed shipping image at `e6172750` and from the `KL_pp_shadow` 1x1 OOC. Use #234's recipe (`docs/testing/PP_SHADOW_BASELINE_RECIPE.md`, `syn/ooc/pp_baseline.py`), and reuse a routed checkpoint that matches the tree when one exists. Vivado runs only under `flock $VIVADO_LOCK`, so wait for the lock. #661, the second pin adoption, is in flight: add its measured delta from that lane's evidence when published, without waiting for it.
2. **Inventory.** For each hierarchy over 500 LUT, state:
   - what protocol function it serves, with the clause;
   - how it is built: register tables, LUT muxes, wide compares, or engines replicated per stream, port or controller;
   - which of its cost is set by Milan or 1722.1 and which is an implementation choice.
3. **Levers.** For each redesign lever give:
   - the estimated LUT saving, with its basis (a measured OOC of a prototype or a closely analogous module beats a hand estimate);
   - the risk;
   - the verification cost: which suites and campaigns re-measure;
   - any protocol-visible effect (none is allowed).

   Consider at least these:
   - time-multiplexing per-protocol engines onto shared datapaths;
   - moving slow-path control (descriptor serving, MAAP, the SRP declaration bookkeeping, ACMP state) to the on-chip RISC-V, under the software-defined end-station direction;
   - BRAM in place of LUT or FF tables;
   - width narrowing derived from the entity model;
   - the remaining #233 items (the VLAN `REFCNT_W_P`, `DESC_LINE_BYTES_P`, FIFO depths);
   - removing diagnostic-only logic from the shipping build, keeping it build-selectable.
4. **Plan.** Give an ordered lane sequence that reaches the target with margin by 2026-12-15. For each lane give its LUT target, files (parent or processor), verification, risks, and dependencies. Name what must hold throughout:
   - every suite, campaign and compliance check at its counts;
   - ATDECC as the only source of state;
   - the second-port redundancy path kept open;
   - the resource gate re-recorded only in the lane that reaches the target.
5. **Document.** Write `docs/design/MARK_II_AREA_PLAN.md` with the baseline, inventory, levers and plan, and commit it with any measurement scripts useful beyond this stage.

**STOP** with the plan's summary and the head. The manager rules on the lane sequence before any RTL work. Do not push.

Do not edit or delete any existing comment.

[A10] **Owner, 2026-10-05, on D9:** the out-of-fabric slow path (lever L2) gets its own milestone, [**Mark II: out-of-fabric slow path** (milestone 13)](https://github.com/kebag-logic/milan-fpga/milestone/13). It is not part of milestone 12, whose plan stays as decided above.

The milestone description records:
- the scope;
- the estimated saving;
- the four normative statements the owner changes before it starts (REQUIREMENTS section 1, NFR-SCOUT-02, NFR-SCOUT-03 and the ownership rule).

It has no due date yet.

[A10] **Owner decision 2026-10-09 15:18, for the Mark II plan (PR #698):** the firmware gets more block RAM. F5's sizing preflight found 147,360 bytes for the largest shape with two interfaces before AECP, against the ~128 KB budget ([#665 6081556432](https://github.com/kebag-logic/milan-fpga/issues/665#issuecomment-6081556432)).

The F5 limit is now 224 KB, about 50 RAMB36 tiles. The plan's block-RAM ledger must:
- price the firmware at up to 50 tiles, plus the measured image at each checkpoint;
- keep the 10 % reserve;
- state which fabric block RAM gives way if the firmware needs more.

If round 1c does not carry this, the next plan round adds it.

[A10] **Round 1d of the Mark II plan** for [A540] on PR #698, head `39258a14` (round 1c, pushed). Round 1c started before the owner's block-RAM decision ([6081706413](https://github.com/kebag-logic/milan-fpga/issues/640#issuecomment-6081706413)) and does not carry it.

1. **Firmware block RAM.** Fold the decision into the plan and AREA_BUDGET.md:
   - the firmware gets up to about 50 RAMB36 tiles (224 KB at 4.5 KB per tile), not the ~128 KB figure;
   - add F5's measured preflight (#665 [6081556432](https://github.com/kebag-logic/milan-fpga/issues/665#issuecomment-6081556432)): largest shape with two interfaces 147,360 B before AECP, shipping shape 94,688 B;
   - add a RAMB36 ledger line: the base record's RAMB36 use, Mark II's fabric changes, the firmware allocation, and the 10 % reserve;
   - name the fabric block RAM that gives way if the firmware needs more than 50 tiles;
   - the default flip and M0s report RAMB36 use against that ledger.
2. Re-run the ledger arithmetic if any figure changes. Keep sentences short and references linked.

Docs only. Commit on `39258a14` with no rebase or amend. Post REVIEW READY with the head.

[A10] **Lane M8a for #640: on-chip control memory for the split placement** (plan L11a; `docs/design/MARK_II_AREA_PLAN.md`, "L11 The SoC side" and "Firmware and block RAM ledger"; D4). Executor [A594], reviewers [R606] (internal) and [R607] (external). Branch `640-m8a` from dev `e8454e27`.

**Scope (plan L11a).**
- In the split placement build, selected by its existing build switch, replace DDR3 main memory with on-chip block RAM for the control firmware:
  - reuse the BIOS ROM and SRAM where the ledger credits them;
  - the firmware allocation fits within 50 RAMB36, including stack, static pools, shape-sized saved-state staging and the descriptor image;
  - the lane owns the memory-map migration and both placement contracts.
- **Ruling:** the all-fabric shipping default keeps its memory system unchanged. Milestone 3 is the all-fabric image, and dropping DDR3 from the default is the flip's decision. Its netlist must be unchanged (digest-identical). Report, as measurement only, what the all-fabric image would need to drop DDR3 too.
- No CPU change. D5's smaller core is lane M8b.
- M2 (#710) and F-INT (#704) also edit `sw/litex/milan_soc.py`, in the MAC crossings and the mailbox. Keep this lane's edits to the memory map and memory instantiation so that the merge stays mechanical.
- Target: about 1,000 LUTs (400-1,500) in the split placement, as a routed delta that replaces the plan's packing assumptions.

**Proof.**
- **Memory fit:** the linked firmware for the shipping 1x1 and the largest supported shape. Report:
  - actual usable bytes against allocated primitives (RAMB36 and RAMB18, as mapped);
  - the BIOS ROM and SRAM reuse count confirmed at your checkpoint;
  - the result against the 224 KB / 50-tile ledger.

  A shape that does not fit is a STOP with the table.
- **Service:**
  - `check_nvm_capture.py` 8x8 at or under 24.5 ms on the new memory;
  - firmware host tests, `nvm_cosim` and `nvm_capture_cpu`;
  - the builder and deploy gates;
  - a LiteX simulation boot of the split image to the control application's ready state.
- **Planted controls**, each failing by name: an allocation over budget, a memory-map overlap, and a staging buffer sized for the wrong shape.
- **Route:** the integrated route of the split placement through the M0s recipe, against M0s's split figure:
  - LUT, FF and RAMB deltas;
  - every endpoint at or above the WNS floor with no exception;
  - the all-fabric endpoint unchanged.

  Vivado runs queue serially under the exclusive lock. The ledger rows are updated with measured values.

The bench boot and persistence checks come after merge. Post TAKEN, then REVIEW READY (or STOP) with the head.
